            <ArticleTitle>Ureteropelvic junction obstruction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>337-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Physicians sometimes forget nongastrointestinal causes of abdominal pain. Yet, abdominal pain was the major complaint of three patients with ureteropelvic junction obstruction (UPJ). Definitive diagnosis can be made either by a &quot;hydration&quot; intravenous pyelogram or by an intravenous pyelogram during an attack.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
